conductor for Fedora and Basalt OS
==================================

Installing the package creates the "conductor" system user (sysusers.d),
/etc/conductor (root:conductor 0750) with tls/ (root:conductor 0750) and
credentials/ (root 0700), and /var/lib/conductor (conductor 0700). It does
not enable or start anything (the distribution's preset disables the
units). Where the targeted SELinux policy is installed, conductor-selinux
comes with it: conductor runs in conductor_t, conductor-helper in
conductor_helper_t.

On the domain controller (Fedora's samba-dc):

  1. Put the web certificate in /etc/conductor/tls/ (cert.pem 0644,
     key.pem 0640 root:conductor), or plan for a reverse proxy.
  2. sudo conductor setup        (interactive; "conductor setup -h")
  3. sudo systemctl enable --now conductor-helper conductor
  4. sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8443/tcp && sudo firewall-cmd --reload

/etc/conductor/conductor.toml is written by "conductor setup" and is not
managed by the package; /etc/conductor/helper.toml is a %config(noreplace)
file (an edited copy is kept on upgrade, the new one lands beside it as
helper.toml.rpmnew).

Another listen port than 8443 needs its SELinux port type:
  sudo semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp <port>

Upgrades restart the services that are running. "dnf remove conductor"
stops and disables the units and removes the packaged files; it keeps what
was created after installation: conductor.toml, the TOTP key in
credentials/, the TLS files, /var/lib/conductor (database and audit log)
and the system user. Delete them by hand when the data is no longer needed
(back up the TOTP key and the database first, see docs/restore.md).

Full guide: https://github.com/openbasalt/samba-conductor/blob/main/docs/install-fedora.md
